\section{Geometric Series}

\lessonobjective{recognize, test, and sum geometric series and interpret the common ratio.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Repeated proportional change creates the simplest infinite series}]

A geometric series multiplies each term by the same ratio. That repeated scaling makes the partial sums algebraically manageable: multiplying the finite sum by the ratio lines up almost every term for cancellation. The resulting formula reveals both convergence and the exact sum.



The ratio controls everything. When \(|r|<1\), powers of \(r\) shrink to zero and the partial sums settle. When \(|r|\ge1\), the terms do not approach zero, so the series cannot converge. In applications, the ratio may represent retained energy after each bounce, a repeated discount, a reflection coefficient, or the fraction of material remaining after each stage.

\end{bgexposition}

\begin{bgbridge}[title={Repeated scaling creates an exact formula}]
A geometric series multiplies each term by a constant ratio \(r\). Multiplying a finite partial sum by \(r\) shifts its terms, so subtraction leaves only the first term and a final remainder.

The condition \(|r|<1\) means that remainder vanishes. When \(|r|\ge1\), the terms do not shrink to zero, so convergence is impossible.
\end{bgbridge}

\begin{bgvisual}{u4a-geometric-self-similarity}{The remaining tail is a scaled copy}{Lengths one-half, one-quarter, one-eighth, and so on fill a unit interval.}
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=11cm,y=.7cm]
\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);\fill[BGBlue!40] (0,0) rectangle (.5,1);\fill[BGGreen!40] (.5,0) rectangle (.75,1);\fill[BGGold!45] (.75,0) rectangle (.875,1);\fill[BGPurple!35] (.875,0) rectangle (.9375,1);
\node at (.25,.5) {$1/2$};\node at (.625,.5) {$1/4$};\node at (.8125,.5) {$1/8$};\node at (.96,.5) {$\cdots$};
\end{tikzpicture}\end{bgvisual}

\begin{bgproofidea}[title={Finite identity first, limit second}]
For \(S_N=a+ar+\cdots+ar^N\), subtraction gives
\[
(1-r)S_N=a-ar^{N+1}.
\]
Thus \(S_N=a(1-r^{N+1})/(1-r)\). Only after this finite formula is established do we let \(N\to\infty\).
\end{bgproofidea}

\begin{bgvisual}{u4a-geometric-areas}{A geometric series repeatedly takes the same fraction}{Successive pieces occupy one half of what remains, producing areas \(1/2,1/4,1/8,\ldots\) whose total fills one whole square.}
