\section{The nth-Term Test for Divergence}

\lessonobjective{use the necessary condition \(a_n\to0\) to rule out convergence and explain why the converse fails.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Every convergent series must eventually add almost nothing}]

If the partial sums \(s_N\) converge, then consecutive partial sums approach the same limit. Their difference \(s_N-s_{N-1}=a_N\) must therefore approach zero. This gives the quickest possible divergence test: if the terms do not approach zero, the series cannot converge.



The test is one-way. Terms approaching zero are necessary but not sufficient because infinitely many tiny positive contributions may still accumulate without bound. The harmonic series is the canonical warning. Writing ``\(a_n\to0\), therefore the series converges'' is not a small technical error; it confuses a necessary condition with a sufficient one.

\end{bgexposition}

\begin{bgbridge}[title={A convergent total cannot keep receiving large deposits}]
If a series converges, its partial sums settle near a finite number. The next term is the change from one partial sum to the next, \(a_n=s_n-s_{n-1}\). Two quantities approaching the same limit must have a difference approaching zero, so convergence forces \(a_n\to0\).

This implication is one-way. Small deposits can still accumulate without bound, as the harmonic series demonstrates. The test is therefore a quick divergence detector, not a convergence test: a nonzero or nonexistent term limit ends the problem, while a zero term limit merely tells us to keep investigating.
\end{bgbridge}

\begin{bgvisual}{u4a-terms-versus-partial-sums}{Small terms can still build an unbounded total}{The terms 1/n decrease toward zero while the corresponding harmonic partial sums continue to rise.}
