\item Classify \(\sum(-1)^n/\sqrt n\).

\item Explain why \(\sum(-1)^n\) diverges.

\item Find an alternating series for which magnitudes are not initially decreasing but eventually are.

\item Describe the behavior of odd and even partial sums.

\end{bgexercises}

\section{Absolute and Conditional Convergence}

\lessonobjective{distinguish absolute from conditional convergence and use absolute convergence as a stronger guarantee.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Signs may be essential or irrelevant}]

If \(\sum|a_n|\) converges, then \(\sum a_n\) converges regardless of the signs. This is absolute convergence. The signs are not doing essential work because even the total magnitude is finite. Conditional convergence occurs when cancellation is necessary: \(\sum a_n\) converges but \(\sum|a_n|\) diverges.



The distinction matters beyond classification. Absolutely convergent series can be rearranged without changing their sum. Conditionally convergent series are far more delicate; rearranging terms can change the sum or destroy convergence. Calculus courses usually state this as an advanced warning, while analysis later proves the exact theorems.

\end{bgexposition}

\begin{bgbridge}[title={Separate convergence by size from convergence by cancellation}]
Absolute convergence asks whether the total magnitude \(\sum|a_n|\) is finite. If it is, the original signed series converges regardless of cancellation. Conditional convergence occurs when signs create convergence even though the magnitude series diverges.

This distinction matters because absolute convergence behaves robustly under regrouping and rearrangement, while conditionally convergent series are more delicate. The correct workflow is to test the absolute-value series first; only if it diverges should an alternating or other signed-series test be used to seek conditional convergence.
\end{bgbridge}

\begin{bgproofidea}[title={Absolute convergence controls both positive and negative mass}]
Write \(a_n=a_n^+-a_n^-\), where both parts are nonnegative and each is bounded by \(|a_n|\). If \(\sum|a_n|\) converges, comparison makes both positive-part series finite, so their difference converges.
\end{bgproofidea}

\begin{bgconcept}[title={Classification}]

A series is absolutely convergent if \(\sum|a_n|\) converges. It is conditionally convergent if \(\sum a_n\) converges but \(\sum|a_n|\) diverges.

\end{bgconcept}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Two alternating examples}]

The series \(\sum(-1)^n/n^2\) converges absolutely because \(\sum1/n^2\) converges. The alternating harmonic series \(\sum(-1)^{n-1}/n\) converges conditionally because its absolute-value series is harmonic and diverges.

\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgexample}[title={A logarithmic series converges only by alternation}]
Classify
\[
\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^n}{n\ln n}.
\]
The magnitudes \(1/(n\ln n)\) decrease eventually and approach zero, so the alternating series converges. But
\[
\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac1{n\ln n}
\]
diverges by the Integral Test because \(\int_2^\infty dx/(x\ln x)=\infty\). Therefore the original series converges conditionally.
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgadvanced}[title={Rearrangement is not innocent}]

The Riemann rearrangement theorem says that a conditionally convergent real series can be rearranged to converge to any prescribed real number, or even to diverge. The phenomenon is impossible for finite sums and startling the first time one meets it. Absolute convergence is precisely the condition that restores finite-sum stability.

\end{bgadvanced}

\begin{bgmistake}[title={Alternating does not automatically mean conditional}]
An alternating series such as \(\sum(-1)^n/n^2\) converges absolutely. Conditional convergence requires both convergence of the signed series and divergence of the absolute-value series.
\end{bgmistake}

\begin{bgcheck}{u4a-absolute_and_conditional_convergence-01}{multiple_choice}{conditional}[Quick check]

Classify \(\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{n-1}/n\) as absolute, conditional, or divergent.

\begin{bghint}

First test the alternating series, then test the absolute-value series.

\end{bghint}

\begin{bgreveal}

It converges by alternation, but \(\sum1/n\) diverges, so convergence is conditional.

\end{bgreveal}

\end{bgcheck}

\begin{bgexercises}

\item Classify \(\sum(-1)^n/n^3\).

\item Classify \(\sum(-1)^n/\sqrt n\).

\item Explain why absolute convergence implies convergence.

\item State what can go wrong when conditionally convergent terms are rearranged.

\end{bgexercises}

\section{The Ratio Test}

\lessonobjective{use ratios of consecutive terms to detect factorial and exponential behavior.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Consecutive ratios expose multiplicative growth}]

Factorials and exponentials are awkward for comparison term by term but simple under division by the preceding term. The Ratio Test measures the eventual multiplication factor in term magnitudes. If that factor is below one, the series behaves like a convergent geometric series; if above one, terms fail to shrink fast enough.



A ratio limit equal to one is inconclusive, not evidence of convergence. This happens for many \(p\)-series, so another test must take over. Always apply the ratio to absolute values and simplify before taking the limit. For power series, the ratio test later becomes the standard route to a radius of convergence.

\end{bgexposition}

\begin{bgbridge}[title={Look at the factor relating neighboring terms}]
The Ratio Test is designed for factorials, exponentials, and products whose neighboring terms simplify dramatically. If \(|a_{n+1}/a_n|\to L<1\), then the tail eventually shrinks by a fixed factor smaller than one and is dominated by a geometric series.

The boundary \(L=1\) is genuinely inconclusive, not a weak hint. Both the harmonic series and \(\sum1/n^2\) have ratio limit one, yet one diverges and the other converges. When the test returns one, choose a different method.
\end{bgbridge}

\begin{bgvisual}{u4a-ratio-geometric-decay}{A ratio below one creates geometric domination}{Successive term magnitudes are shown decreasing by a factor bounded above by a fixed number r less than one.}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\foreach \i/\h in {0/3.2,1/2.25,2/1.58,3/1.11,4/.78,5/.55,6/.38}{\fill[BGBlue!25] (\i,0) rectangle +(0.65,\h);\draw[BGBlue] (\i,0) rectangle +(0.65,\h);}
\draw[->] (-.2,0)--(7.2,0) node[right] {$n$};
\node at (3.2,3.7) {each late term is at most $r$ times the previous one};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{bgvisual}

\begin{bgproofidea}[title={A limit below one yields an actual geometric bound}]
Choose \(r\) with \(L<r<1\). Eventually \(|a_{n+1}|\le r|a_n|\), so repeated application gives \(|a_{N+k}|\le |a_N|r^k\). The tail is bounded by a convergent geometric series.
\end{bgproofidea}

\begin{bgconcept}[title={Ratio Test}]

Let
\[
L=\lim_{n\to\infty}\left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|.
\]
If \(L<1\), \(\sum a_n\) converges absolutely. If \(L>1\) or \(L=\infty\), it diverges. If \(L=1\), the test is inconclusive.

\end{bgconcept}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Factorial in the denominator}]

For \(a_n=3^n/n!\),
\[
\left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|
=\frac{3^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\frac{n!}{3^n}
=\frac3{n+1}\to0.
\]
Therefore \(\sum3^n/n!\) converges absolutely.

\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgexample}[title={Factorials collapse under neighboring ratios}]
Test
\[
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{3^n}{n!}.
\]
Let \(a_n=3^n/n!\). Then
\[
\left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|
=\frac{3^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\frac{n!}{3^n}
=\frac3{n+1}\to0.
\]
Since \(0<1\), the series converges absolutely.
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgmistake}[title={L equals one means stop using this test}]
A ratio limit of one gives no conclusion. Do not label it “barely convergent” or “probably divergent.” Switch tests.
\end{bgmistake}

\begin{bgcheck}{u4a-ratio_test-01}{expression}{0}[Quick check]

For \(a_n=3^n/n!\), find \(\lim|a_{n+1}/a_n|\).

\begin{bghint}

Cancel \(3^n\) and \(n!\).

\end{bghint}

\begin{bgreveal}

\[\frac3{n+1}\to0.\]

\end{bgreveal}

\end{bgcheck}

\begin{bgexercises}

\item Apply the Ratio Test to \(\sum n!/4^n\).

\item Apply it to \(\sum n^2/5^n\).

\item Show why the Ratio Test is inconclusive for \(\sum1/n^2\).

\item Explain the geometric-series intuition behind \(L<1\).

\end{bgexercises}

\section{Choosing a Convergence Test}

\lessonobjective{choose an efficient convergence test from the visible structure of a series and justify the choice.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={The best test is the one matched to the structure}]

A convergence problem is partly classification and partly strategy. Before calculating, inspect the term. Is it geometric? Does it telescope? Do the terms fail to approach zero? Is there a familiar \(p\)-series scale? Are signs alternating? Are factorials or whole expressions raised to \(n\) present? This inspection prevents pages of algebra using a test that was never appropriate.



More than one test may work, but some tests provide better information. Alternating-series reasoning gives an error bound; absolute convergence gives stability; integral estimates quantify tails; geometric recognition gives an exact sum. A complete solution names the test, verifies its hypotheses, performs the decisive calculation, and states convergence type when signs are involved.

\end{bgexposition}

\begin{bgbridge}[title={Test selection is structural diagnosis}]
A convergence problem is not solved by cycling through every theorem. Begin with the term limit, then inspect the formula for recognizable structure: geometric ratios, telescoping differences, reciprocal powers, positive comparisons, alternation, factorials, or whole-term nth powers.

The best test is usually the one that exposes the dominant mechanism with the least algebra. Several tests may work, but a good solution explains why the chosen one fits. When a test is inconclusive, record that fact accurately and move to a method designed for the remaining structure.
\end{bgbridge}

\begin{bgvisual}{u4a-test-selection-tree}{Choose a convergence test from the series structure}{A flowchart begins with the term limit, then branches to geometric, telescoping, positive-term comparison, alternating, factorial, and nth-power structures.}
