\end{bgvisual}

\begin{bgconcept}[title={A practical hierarchy}]

Try, in order when appropriate: recognize exact forms; apply the nth-term test; compare dominant positive behavior; use alternation; use ratio/root for factorial or exponential structure; use the Integral Test when a natural decreasing function and remainder estimate are valuable.

\end{bgconcept}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Classify a mixed series}]

For
\[
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(-1)^{n-1}\frac{n}{n^2+1},
\]
the magnitudes behave like \(1/n\), decrease eventually, and approach zero. The Alternating Series Test gives convergence. Limit comparison of absolute values with \(1/n\) shows the convergence is conditional.

\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgexample}[title={A complete decision path for a signed series}]
Classify
\[
\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}(-1)^n\frac{n}{n^2+1}.
\]
First, the terms approach zero, so the divergence test is inconclusive. For absolute convergence,
\[
\frac{n}{n^2+1}\sim\frac1n,
\]
and limit comparison with \(1/n\) gives divergence. The magnitudes decrease eventually and approach zero, so the Alternating Series Test proves convergence. Therefore the series converges conditionally.
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgadvanced}[title={No finite list of tests is complete}]

Convergence tests are sufficient criteria tailored to common structures. There is no promise that one named classroom test will classify every series presented in research or even every series one can write down. The deeper subject studies Cauchy criteria, summability methods, asymptotic expansions, and modes of convergence for functions.

\end{bgadvanced}

\begin{bgmistake}[title={An inconclusive test is not a failed solution}]
Correctly recognizing that a test gives no conclusion is useful information. The error is forcing a verdict from it rather than selecting the next appropriate test.
\end{bgmistake}

\begin{bgcheck}{u4a-choosing_a_convergence_test-01}{multiple_choice}{ratio}[Quick check]

Which test is most natural for \(\sum n!/5^n\): comparison, alternating, ratio, or integral?

\begin{bghint}

Look for factorials and consecutive-term cancellation.

\end{bghint}

\begin{bgreveal}

The Ratio Test is the natural choice.

\end{bgreveal}

\end{bgcheck}

